[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=295777)[0m 3. \(1 + \frac{i}{2} = \frac{2+i}{2} = 1 + \frac{i}{2}\)
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=295777)[0m 4. \(1 - \frac{1}{4} = \frac{3}{4}\)
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=295777)[0m 5. \(1 - \frac{1}{16} = \frac{15}{16}\)
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=295777)[0m 6. \(1 - \frac{1}{256}\)
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=295777)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=295777)[0m We can see that the terms will continue to be small for large \(k\), and their product will be close to 1.
