[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m We can see that the integrand is a simple function of the form \( \frac{1}{z - z_0} \), where \( z = t \) and \( z_0 = a - bi \). This is a well-known result in complex analysis, where the integral of \( \frac{1}{z - z_0} \) over a real axis can be evaluated using contour integration. Specifically, if \( z_0 \) is outside the contour, the integral evaluates to zero; otherwise, it evaluates to \( 2\pi i \) times the residue at \( z_0 \).
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m In our case, \( z_0 = a - bi \). Since \( b > 0 \), the point \( z_0 \) lies in the upper half-plane. When \( R \) goes to infinity, the contour will enclose the point \( z_0 \), and hence the integral will evaluate to \( 2\pi i \times 1 = 2\pi i \).
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m Now, we can directly take the limit:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m \[
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m \lim_{R \to \infty} \frac{1}{2\pi i} \int_{-R}^R \frac{1}{t - (a - bi)} \, dt
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m = \frac{1}{2\pi i} \cdot 2\pi i = 1
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m boxed{1}
