[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=295775)[0m [2026-04-06 22:44:00] [math_rule_reward_worker.py (162)] [ERROR] [reward-math_rule-1 1 / 4][PID 295775] timeout answer: 211, response: To determine the least integer \( b \geq 2 \) for which there are more than ten \( b \)-beautiful integers, we start by expressing a \( b \)-beautiful integer \( n \) in base \( b \). A \( b \)-beautiful integer has exactly two digits, so it can be written as \( n = d_1 d_0 \) in base \( b \), where \( d_1 \) and \( d_0 \) are the digits and \( d_1 \neq 0 \). This means \( n = d_1 b + d_0 \) and the condition given is \( d_1 + d_0 = \sqrt{n} \).
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=295775)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=295775)[0m First, we square both sides of the equation \( d_1 + d_0 = \sqrt{n} \) to get:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=295775)[0m \[ n = (d_1 + d_0)^2. \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=295775)[0m Since \( n = d_1 b + d_0 \), we substitute and get:
