[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m First, consider the case when \( n \leq m \). The difference \( f_n - f_m \) is non-zero only on the interval \(\left[\frac{1}{m}, \frac{1}{n}\right]\). On this interval, we have:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m \[ f_n(x) = 1 - nx \quad \text{and} \quad f_m(x) = 1 - mx, \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m so
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m \[ f_n(x) - f_m(x) = (1 - nx) - (1 - mx) = (m - n)x. \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m The \( L^p \) norm of \( f_n - f_m \) is then:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m \[ \lVert f_n - f_m \rVert_p = \left( \int_0^1 |f_n(x) - f_m(x)|^p \, dx \right)^{1/p} = \left( \int_{1/m}^{1/n} (m - n)^p x^p \, dx \right)^{1/p}. \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m Evaluating the integral, we get:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m \[ \int_{1/m}^{1/n} (m - n)^p x^p \, dx = (m - n)^p \int_{1/m}^{1/n} x^p \, dx = (m - n)^p \left[ \frac{x^{p+1}}{p+1} \right]_{1/m}^{1/n} = (m - n)^p \left( \frac{1}{(p+1)n^{p+1}} - \frac{1}{(p+1)m^{p+1}} \right). \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m Since \( n \leq m \), we have:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m \[ \lVert f_n - f_m \rVert_p = (m - n)^{p/(p+1)} \left( \frac{1}{n} - \frac{1}{m} \right)^{1/p} \leq (m - n)^{p/(p+1)} \cdot \frac{1}{n}. \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m For the sequence to be Cauchy, we need \(\lVert f_n - f_m \rVert_p \to 0\) as \( n, m \to \infty \). This will happen if \( p < \infty \), because then \((m - n)^{p/(p+1)} \to 0\) as \( m, n \to \infty \).
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m Now, we need to check if the sequence converges in \( L^p \). The sequence \(\{f_n\}\) converges pointwise to the zero function on \([0,1]\). To check convergence in \( L^p \), we need to see if \(\lVert f_n \rVert_p \to 0\) as \( n \to \infty \). We have:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m \[ \lVert f_n \rVert_p = \left( \int_0^1 |f_n(x)|^p \, dx \right)^{1/p} = \left( \int_0^{1/n} (1 - nx)^p \, dx \right)^{1/p}. \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m Evaluating the integral, we get:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m \[ \int_0^{1/n} (1 - nx)^p \, dx = \left[ \frac{(1 - nx)^{p+1}}{-(p+1)n} \right]_0^{1/n} = \frac{1}{(p+1)n} - \frac{0}{(p+1)n} = \frac{1}{(p+1)n}. \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m So,
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m \[ \lVert f_n \rVert_p = \left( \frac{1}{(p+1)n} \right)^{1/p} = \frac{1}{(p+1)^{1/p} n^{1/p}}. \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=295776)[0m As \( n \to \infty \), \(\lVert f_n \rVert_p \to 0\) if \( p > 1 \). Therefore, the sequence converges to the zero function in \( L^p \) for \( p > 1 \).
